  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated showers and thunderstorms this afternoon. \nDrier conditions following today's cold front.\n\nSatellite shows continued clearing over southern New England today, \nwhich has aided in the development of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. As of the time of writing, the cold front is passing \nthrough western MA and CT based on latest observations towards the \nBerkshires and in north central CT. Ahead of the front, showers and \nthunderstorms have popped up across central MA, with an earlier \nround having gone through northern MA. These will continue to push \neastward and will likely maintain their growth given the enhanced \nSBCAPE, MLCAPE, clearer skies, and warmer surface temperatures in \nthe 80s with dew points climbing into the low 70s/upper 60s. Strong \nto damaging wind gusts remain a possibility given DCAPE values \nbetween 1000-1100 J/kg. These showers and thunderstorms should exit \nthe region early tonight, generally around 8-9 PM. Some may linger a \nbit longer near the Cape and Islands, though.\n\nAs the cold front moves through the region today and tonight, winds \nwill shift more NW, and drier air will move in with cooler \ntemperatures. High pressure starts to build in from the west \nThursday while an upper level trough moves over the northeast. Highs \nThursday will reach the low to mid 80s given 925 mb temperatures \naround 16-18z. With high pressure building in, winds will slacken \nand seabreezes will likely develop tomorrow. Mostly clear skies and \nlight to calm winds Thursday night will lend to radiational cooling \nas well, leading to lows dropping into the 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures through the \nweekend. \n\nWhile the synoptic pattern will feature broad, weak troughing in the \nmid levels over the northeast, subsidence associated with a building \nsurface high pressure center will keep things dry from Friday \nthrough at least Sunday. This, while said troughing pattern keeps \nseasonable temperatures around; 850 mb temps remain in the 11-13C \nrange clear through early next week, which will lead to high \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to low 80s each day. Behind today's \ncold front, dewpoints will drop into the 40s and low 50s through \nSunday as well, making it feel less humid and more comfortable. \nAltogether this should be a fantastic stretch of weather for outdoor \nplans. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Rain and thunderstorm chances return for the first \nhalf of next week. Seasonable temperatures continue. \n\nThe pattern begins to break down around Monday with the mid level \ntrough digging into the northeast with several disturbances rotating \naround the base of the trough inducing areas of surface low pressure \nwhich will stand the chance to bring us rain and thunderstorms as \nsoon as Monday with the threat continuing much of next week. As \nmentioned previously, there is no big warm-up on the horizon.",
